# Copy to `.env` NEXT TO compose.yaml on the droplet. Never commit the real thing.
#
#   install -m 600 /dev/null .env    # create it 0600 BEFORE writing secrets into it
#
# These stay on the host on purpose. A secret routed through CI is a secret in that pipeline's
# logs, its cache, and the context of every pull request from a fork.
#
# The CATALOGUE is not here and is no longer a file at all: it lives in the SQLite database on the
# `regserve-data` volume. `seed.json`, next to this file, is imported into it once — on the first
# boot with an empty database — and ignored from then on. See docs/operations/deployment.md. A
# missing seed.json fails `compose up`, on purpose.
#
# Everything here is read except REGSERVE_TOKEN_PEPPER and the OAuth pair, which are read TOGETHER
# or not at all -- see the section on them below.

# The subdomain Traefik routes to this service.
REGSERVE_HOST=nparseplugins.prokopto.dev

# The external Docker network Traefik watches. `docker network ls` if you are not sure.
TRAEFIK_NETWORK=proxy
TRAEFIK_ENTRYPOINT_HTTP=http
TRAEFIK_ENTRYPOINT_HTTPS=https
TRAEFIK_CERTRESOLVER=http

# The image the deploy workflow writes here. Leave it; CI rewrites this line on each deploy.
REGSERVE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/prokopto-dev/nparse-plugin-regserve:latest

# Consumed: the server reads this when --log-level is not passed. debug, info, warn or error.
REGSERVE_LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Sign-in, and the secrets it needs --------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE THREE BELOW ARE SET TOGETHER OR NOT AT ALL. With none of them set, the server serves the
# catalogue and does not register the sign-in routes at all -- an honest 404 rather than a login
# button that leads to a 500. With SOME of them set the server REFUSES TO START, because somebody
# has configured half an OAuth application and both quiet outcomes are worse than a boot failure.
#
# REGSERVE_PUBLIC_URL must be https. The session cookie is `__Host-` prefixed, which browsers
# refuse over http, so sign-in over http would appear to work and leave the user signed out. The
# server checks this at boot rather than letting it be discovered in a browser console.
#
# The database path is set in compose.yaml rather than here (REGSERVE_DB_PATH=/data/regserve.db):
# it names a path inside the container, which is a property of the image and the volume, not a
# per-host secret or preference.

# Keys every credential hash in the database -- PATs and browser sessions alike. Generate once with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# ROTATING THIS INVALIDATES EVERY ISSUED TOKEN AND SIGNS EVERYBODY OUT, because the stored value
# is HMAC-SHA256(pepper, secret) and there is no way to re-derive it. Every plugin pipeline then
# fails to publish until its owner mints a new token. Treat it as permanent unless it has leaked.
REGSERVE_TOKEN_PEPPER=

# The GitHub OAuth application. GitHub is the only identity provider (ADR-0011), so this is the
# only pair of these. Callback URL: https://$REGSERVE_HOST/auth/github/callback
REGSERVE_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
REGSERVE_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Who may approve releases: comma-separated GitHub handles. These people, and only these people,
# decide what this registry lists.
#
# EMPTY MEANS NOBODY, not everybody. Releases will publish, verify, and sit in the queue until
# somebody is named here; the server warns at boot when the queue is non-empty and this is not set.
#
# A handle grants nothing until that person has signed in here at least once -- the check resolves
# against a proven identity, so a typo grants nobody rather than granting whoever registers that
# name next. Removing a handle takes effect on their next request, with no restart.
#
# There is no column and no endpoint that makes somebody a reviewer. A row would be one somebody
# can UPDATE at 2am; an endpoint would be an escalation path. Reviewing is also capability-floor,
# so no personal access token can approve a release however it is scoped.
REGSERVE_REVIEWERS=
